cture is completely broken (can't identify gates), fall back to simulation.\n\nLet me just code this up and test it. I'll implement the structural algorithm and then use simulation for verification/finding remaining swaps.\n\nActually, let me think about what the problem really is and look for the simplest possible correct solution.\n\nThe circuit is a ripple-carry adder. Here's the key insight: **each gate's in1/in2 fields are correct** (only out is swapped). So the gate INPUTS tell me the correct structure.\n\nGiven that, I can build the expected wiring by following inputs:\n\nFor bit i > 0:\n- Let expected_ti = XOR(xi, yi).out — this is the wire that SHOULD carry xi XOR yi, but might have been swapped.\n- Let expected_ai = AND(xi, yi).out — this is the wire that SHOULD carry xi AND yi, but might have been swapped.\n\nNow, the downstream gates use wire NAMES. So consumers of expected_ti read that NAME and get whatever value is on it (could be correct or wrong).\n\nFor the circuit to work correctly for bit i:\n- XOR(xi, yi) should produce a wire that feeds XOR(expected_ti, c_{i-1}) and AND(expected_ti, c_{i-1}).\n  - If expected_ti ≠ the wire consumed by these gates, then expected_ti is swapped.\n  \nHmm, the consumers of expected_ti read expected_ti BY NAME. So if I search for gates whose inputs include expected_ti, I'll find the downstream gates (regardless of what value is on the wire).\n\nSo my algorithm should be:\n1. Find XOR(xi, yi). Output = wire_ti.\n2. Find AND(xi, yi). Output = wire_ai.\n3. Find all gates whose inputs include wire_ti. There should be exactly 2: XOR and AND, both reading wire_ti.\n   - If exactly 2 are found, their other input is the carry wire c_{i-1}.\n   - If fewer or more are found, something is wrong (might be a swap involving wire_ti).\n\nBut wait — the gates consuming wire_ti are correct because they read the wire by name. The issue is whether wire_ti actually carries the correct value (xi XOR yi). If there's a swap involving wire_