on w1 or w2 are wrong when v1 ≠ v2.\n\nFor specific test inputs, I can check which z bits are wrong. But I need to know the expected value of each wire to detect swaps.\n\nHmm, let me think about this from yet another angle. What if I use the structure to determine what each wire SHOULD be, and then compare with what it IS?\n\n**Approach: Reconstruct correct circuit structure.**\n\nGiven the gates, I know the correct structure: each gate's op and inputs are correct. Only the outputs might be swapped. So:\n\n1. The gate XOR(xi, yi) computes xi XOR yi. Its output carries this value.\n2. The wire that carries xi XOR yi should be called \"ti\" (by convention, though the actual name is random).\n\nWait, the internal wires DON'T follow a naming convention. So I can't check if the wire that carries xi XOR yi is \"correctly\" named.\n\nBut I CAN check: is the wire that carries xi XOR yi connected to the right consumers?\n- It should be connected to XOR(ti, c_{i-1}) and AND(ti, c_{i-1}).\n- If XOR(xi, yi).out is consumed by these two gates, the wiring is correct for that bit.\n- If not, the wire was swapped.\n\nThis is the structural approach I had before. Let me implement it properly.\n\n**Algorithm:**\n\n1. Group gates by the pair of input wires.\n2. Create a mapping from wire → gates that produce/consume it.\n\n3. For bit 0:\n   - Find gates with inputs x00, y00.\n   - XOR should produce z00. If XOR.out ≠ z00, XOR.out and z00 are swapped.\n   - AND produces carry wire c0.\n\n4. For bit i > 0:\n   - XOR(xi, yi).out = w_ti (wire carrying xi XOR yi)\n   - AND(xi, yi).out = w_ai (wire carrying xi AND yi)\n   \n   - Find gates where one input is w_ti:\n     - XOR gate → output should be zi. xor_wt.out → check if = zi.\n     - AND gate → output = propagate wire.\n   \n   - Find gate with inputs w_ai and propagate:\n     - OR gate → output = carry wire ci.\n\n   - Check: if xor_wt.out ≠ zi, then xor_wt.out and zi are swapped.\n\n5. Continue up to max bit.\n\n6. Check if final carry wire from last bit should be z(max+1):\n   - If last carry wire ≠ z(max+1) and z(max+1) is produced by a gate:\n     - z(max+1) might be swapped.\n   - Actually, z(max+1) should just be the carry from the last bit.\n\n7. Return sorted set of swapped wires.\n\nBut this assumes that the carry wire from bit 0 feeds correctly into bit 1. If the carry wire is swapped, then the inputs to bit 1's gates are wrong.\n\nWait, the carry wire is a specific wire name (say \"c00\"). Even if it's swapped, the wire \"c00\" still exists and the gates that consume it still read from \"c00\". The only difference is that \"c00\" now carries a different value (because the gate producing it was swapped).\n\nBut for my structural analysis, I don't care about values — I only care about wire names. So even if \"c00\" is swapped, I can still find XOR(ti, c00) and AND(ti, c00) by looking for gates whose inputs include \"c00\".\n\nSo the structural analysis WORKS even with swapped wires! The swaps only change out fields, not in fields. So the connectivity is always correct.\n\nBUT: what if the wire that's swapped IS the ti wire or the carry wire itself? Then:\n- XOR(xi,yi).out = w_ti might not be the same as the wire consumed by the downstream XOR/AND gates.\n- But wait, the downstream gates consume w_ti by name. So they DO consume XOR(xi,yi).out.\n\nUnless XOR(xi,yi).out was swapped! Then:\n- XOR(xi,yi).out = w_other (not w_ti)\n- w_ti is now produced by some other gate\n\nIn this case:\n- Gates consuming w_ti still read from wire named w_ti.\n- But now w_ti is produced by a different gate, not XOR(xi,yi).\n- XOR(xi,yi)'s output (w_other) is consumed by gates that should have consumed w_other.\n\nSo:\n- Consumers of XOR(xi,yi).out vs consumers of w_ti: these are DIFFERENT sets of gates.\n- XOR(xi,yi).out is consumed by gates that should be consuming w_ti (the swapped partner).\n- w_ti is consumed by gates that should be consuming XOR(xi,yi).out (the swapped partner's original consumers).\n\nSo if I search for gates consuming XOR(xi,yi).out, I'll find the wrong gates. My structural analysis would break.\n\nThis is the fundamental problem. Let me handle it differently.\n\n**Alternative: Identify wires by their role, not their name.**\n\nSince I know the circuit is a ripple-carry adder, I can determine the role of each gate based on its operation and inputs, independent of its output wire name.\n\nFor each gate, based on its op and inputs, I can classify it:\n- If op = 'XOR' and inputs = {xi, yi} → this is the \"partial sum\" gate for bit i.\n- If op = 'AND' and inputs = {xi, yi} → this is the \"generate\" gate for bit i.\n- If op = 'XOR' and inputs are two non-x/non-y wires → this is the \"output sum\" gate.\n- If op = 'AND' and inputs are two non-x/non-y wires → this is the \"propagate\" gate.\n- If op = 'OR' → this is the \"carry out\" gate.\n\nThe hitch is determining which bit each \"non-xy\" gate belongs to. But I can figure this out by following connectivity.\n\nFor bit 0:\n- XOR(x0, y0) → partial sum (should be z00)\n- AND(x0, y0) → generate (carry)\n\nFor bit 1:\n- XOR(x1, y1) → partial sum. Output goes to some wire w.\n- The carry from bit 0 is AND(x0,y0).out.\n- Gates whose inputs are {w, carry_from_0}: XOR and AND.\n- The XOR's output should go to z01. The AND's output is the propagate.\n- Gates whose inputs are {generate_from_1, propagate}: OR.\n- The OR's output is the carry from bit 1.\n\nI can recursively identify the structure! The key is that the CARRY wires connect adjacent bits.\n\nNow, to detect swaps: for each gate, I know its role and its expected output wire. If the actual output wire doesn't match, it's swapped.\n\nFor example:\n- XOR(xi, yi) should output to the wire that feeds XOR(xor_out, carry_{i-1}) and AND(xor_out, carry_{i-1}).\n  - It should be connected to exactly these two gates (as input).\n  - If it's connected to different gates, the output was swapped.\n\nWait, I just realized this is the same approach I described 