ther is the carry from bit N-1\n\nFor each x/y pair (xNN, yNN):\n- There should be an XOR gate with these inputs (partial sum)\n- There should be an AND gate with these inputs (generate)\n\nNow, when I trace the expected structure and find a mismatch, I flag the wires involved.\n\nLet me just implement this. I think the cleanest approach is:\n\n1. Build `wireProducedBy` (Map<string, Gate>): for each wire, which gate outputs to it.\n2. Build `wireUsedBy` (Map<string, Gate[]>): for each wire, which gates use it as input.\n3. Find all gates with xNN/yNN inputs (same N):\n   - XOR → `psums[N]` (partial sum wire)\n   - AND → `gens[N]` (generate wire)\n   - These are expected to exist for all N\n\n4. For N=0:\n   - `z00` should be `psums[0]` → if not, `z00` and `psums[0]`/actual output are swapped\n   - `carry[0]` = `gens[0]`\n\n5. For N>0:\n   - Look for XOR gate with inputs `psums[N]` and `carry[N-1]`\n     - If found and its output is `zNN`: correct\n     - If found but output is NOT `zNN`: its output and `zNN` are swapped\n     - If NOT found: look for XOR gate that outputs to `zNN`; its inputs should be `psums[N]` and `carry[N-1]`\n   - Look for AND gate with inputs `psums[N]` and `carry[N-1]`\n     - This gives `props[N]`\n   - Look for OR gate with inputs `gens[N]` and `props[N]`\n     - This gives `carry[N]`\n\n6. For final carry: `carry[maxBit]` should be `z{maxBit+1}`\n\n7. Collect all mismatched wires.\n\nThis is the core algorithm. Let me implement it.\n\nOne nuance: when I say \"look for XOR gate with inputs A and B\", the order of A and B doesn't matter (XOR is commutative). Same for AND and OR.\n\nAlso, when testing with specific inputs to find wrong bits, I could:\n- For a set of known X+Y inputs, simulate the circuit and compare zNN outputs to X+Y.\n- Find which z-bits are consistently wrong.\n- Then trace back from those z-bits to find swapped wires.\n\nBut I think the structural approach is cleaner.\n\nLet me now write the implementation.\n\n```typescript\ninterface Gate {\n  op: 'AND' | 'OR' | 'XOR';\n  in1: string;\n  in2: string;\n  out: string;\n}\n\nfunction simulate(gates: Gate[], inputs: Record<string, number>): Record<string, number> {\n  // Topological sort\n  const values = { ...inputs };\n  const remaining = new Set(gates);\n  const byOut = new Map<string, Gate>();\n  \n  // Track which gates we can process\n  let changed = true;\n  while (remaining.size > 0 && changed) {\n    changed = false;\n    for (const gate of remaining) {\n      if (gate.in1 in values && gate.in2 in values) {\n        const v1 = values[gate.in1];\n        const v2 = values[gate.in2];\n        switch (gate.op) {\n          case 'AND': values[gate.out] = v1 & v2; break;\n          case 'OR': values[gate.out] = v1 | v2; break;\n          case 'XOR': values[gate.out] = v1 ^ v2; break;\n        }\n        remaining.delete(gate);\n        changed = true;\n      }\n    }\n  }\n  \n  return values;\n}\n```\n\nWait, but this naive approach of repeatedly scanning all remaining gates could be O(n²) which is fine for 220 gates. But let me use a queue-based topological sort instead:\n\n```typescript\nfunction simulate(gates: Gate[], inputs: Record<string, number>): Record<string, number> {\n  const values = { ...inputs };\n  \n  // For each wire, list of gates that use it as input\n  const consumers = new Map<string, Gate[]>();\n  // Count of unready inputs for each gate\n  const inDegree = new Map<Gate, number>();\n  const queue: Gate[] = [];\n  \n  for (const gate of gates) {\n    inDegree.set(gate, 2); // each gate has 2 inputs\n    for (const inp of [gate.in1, gate.in2]) {\n      if (!consumers.has(inp)) consumers.set(inp, []);\n      consumers.get(inp)!.push(gate);\n    }\n  }\n  \n  // Initial: gates whose inputs are all provided\n  for (const gate of gates) {\n    const v1 = values[gate.in1];\n    const v2 = values[gate.in2];\n    if (v1 !== undefined && v2 !== undefined) {\n      queue.push(gate);\n    }\n  }\n  \n  while (queue.length > 0) {\n    const gate = queue.shift()!;\n    const v1 = values[gate.in1];\n    const v2 = values[gate.in2];\n    switch (gate.op) {\n      case 'AND': values[gate.out] = v1 & v2; break;\n      case 'OR': values[gate.out] = v1 | v2; break;\n      case 'XOR': values[gate.out] = v1 ^ v2; break;\n    }\n    \n    // Check dependent gates\n    const dependents = consumers.get(gate.out) || [];\n    for (const dep of dependents) {\n      const remaining = (inDegree.get(dep) || 2) - 1;\n      inDegree.set(dep, remaining);\n      if (remaining === 0) {\n        queue.push(dep);\n      }\n    }\n  }\n  \n  return values;\n}\n```\n\nHmm, actually the inDegree approach is a bit tricky because all 2 inputs need to be available. Let me simplify: I'll use a map of how many inputs are ready, decrementing from 2.\n\nActually, the simpler approach of tracking when both inputs become available:\n\n```typescript\nfunction simulate(gates: Gate[], inputs: Record<string, number>): Record<string, number> {\n  const values: Record<string, number> = { ...inputs };\n  \n  // Map from wire to gates that use it\n  const consumers = new Map<string, Gate[]>();\n  for (const g of gates) {\n    for (const w of [g.in1, g.in2]) {\n      if (!consumers.has(w)) consumers.set(w, []);\n      consumers.get(w)!.push(g);\n    }\n  }\n  \n  // Number of unready inputs per gate\n  const needs = new Map<Gate, number>();\n  const queue: Gate[] = [];\n  \n  for (const g of gates) {\n    let n = 0;\n    if (!(g.in1 in values)) n++;\n    if (!(g.in2 in values)) n++;\n    needs.set(g, n);\n    if (n === 0) queue.push(g);\n  }\n  \n  while (queue.length > 0) {\n    const g = queue.shift()!;\n    const v1 = values[g.in1];\n    const v2 = values[g.in2];\n    \n    switch (g.op) {\n      case 'AND': values[g.out] = v1 & v2; break;\n      case 'OR': values[g.out] = v1 | v2; break;\n      case 'XOR': values[g.out] = v1 ^ v2; break;\n    }\n    \n    // Notify consumers\n    for (const dep of (consumers.get(g.out) || [])) {\n      const cur = needs.get(dep)!;\n      needs.set(dep, cur - 1);\n      if (cur - 1 === 0) {\n        queue.push(dep);\n      }\n    }\n  }\n  \n  return values;\n}\n```\n\nThis is correct for acyclic circuits.\n\nNow for `findSwappedWires`, let me implement the structural validation approach:\n\n```typescript\nfunction findSwappedWires(gates: Gate[]): string[] {\n  // Build maps\n  const wireOutputOf = new Map<string, Gate>(); // wire -> gate that outputs to it\n  const wireInputOf = new Map<string, Gate[]>(); // wire -> gates that use it as input\n  \n  for (const g of gates) {\n    wireOutputOf.set(g.out, g);\n    for (const inp of [g.in1, g.in2]) {\n      if (!wireInputOf.has(inp)) wireInputOf.set(inp, []);\n      wireInputOf.get(inp)!.push(g);\n    }\n  }\n  \n  // Find max bit from input wires\n  const allWires = new Set<string>();\n  for (const g of gates) {\n    allWires.add(g.in1);\n    allWires.add(g.in2);\n    allWires.add(g.out);\n  }\n  \n  let maxBit = -1;\n  for (const w of allWires) {\n    const m = w.match(/^x(\\d+)$/);\n    if (m) maxBit = Math.max(maxBit, parseInt(m[1]));\n  }\n  \n  // Find half-adders: for each bit N, XOR(xN, yN) and AND(xN, yN)\n  const psums: (string | null)[] = new Array(maxBit + 1).fill(null); // partial sum wires\n  const gens: (string | null)[] = new Array(maxBit + 1).fill(null); // generate wires\n  const swaps = new Set<string>();\n  \n  for (const g of gates) {\n    const m1 = g.in1.match(/^x(\\d+)$/);\n    const m2 = g.in2.match(/^y(\\d+)$/);\n    if (m1 && m2 && m1[1] === m2[1]) {\n      const bit = parseInt(m1[1]);\n      if (g.op === 'XOR') {\n        psums[bit] = g.out;\n        if (bit === 0 && g.out !== `z00`) {\n          swaps.add(g.out);\n          swaps.add(`z00`);\n        }\n        if (bit > 0 && g.out.startsWith('z')) {\n          swaps.add(g.out);\n        }\n      } else if (g.op === 'AND') {\n        gens[bit] = g.out;\n        if (g.out.startsWith('z')) {\n          swaps.add(g.out);\n        }\n      }\n    }\n  }\n  \n  // Trace carry chain\n  const carries: (string | null)[] = new Array(maxBit + 2).fill(null);\n  carries[0] = gens[0]; // carry from bit 0\n  \n  for (let n = 1; n <= maxBit; n++) {\n    const psum = psums[n];\n    const carryIn = carries[n - 1];\n    const gen = gens[n];\n    \n    if (!psum || !carryIn) continue;\n    \n    // Look for XOR(psum, carryIn) -> should be zNN\n    let foundXor = false;\n    for (const g of gates) {\n      if (g.op === 'XOR' && \n          ((g.in1 === psum && g.in2 === carryIn) || (g.in1 === carryIn && g.in2 === psum))) {\n        foundXor = true;\n        if (g.out !== `z${n.toString().padStart(2, '0')}`) {\n          swaps.add(g.out);\n          swaps.add(`z${n.toString().padStart(2, '0')}`);\n        }\n        break;\n      }\n    }\n    \n    // If not found, check what outputs to zNN\n    if (!foundXor) {\n      const zWire = `z${n.toString().padStart(2, '0')}`;\n      const zGate = wireOutputOf.get(zWire);\n      if (zGate) {\n        // The gate that produces zNN should be XOR(psum, carryIn)\n        // Since it's not, figure out which wire is wrong\n        swaps.add(zWire);\n        if (zGate.in1 === psum || zGate.in2 === psum) {\n          // carryIn is wrong\n          swaps.add(carryIn);\n        } else if (zGate.in1 === carryIn || zGate.in2 === carryIn) {\n          // psum is wrong\n          swaps.add(psum);\n        } else {\n          // both are wrong\n          swaps.add(zGate.in1);\n          swaps.add(zGate.in2);\n        }\n      }\n    }\n    \n    // Look for AND(psum, carryIn) -> propagate\n    let foundAnd = false;\n    for (const g of gates) {\n      if (g.op === 'AND' && \n          ((g.in1 === psum && g.in2 === carryIn) || (g.in1 === carryIn && g.in2 === psum))) {\n        foundAnd = true;\n        if (g.out.startsWith('z') && g.out !== `z${(maxBit + 1).toString().padStart(2, '0')}`) {\n          swaps.add(g.out);\n        }\n        \n        // Now look for OR(gen, propagate) -> carry\n        const prop = g.out;\n        for (const orGate of gates) {\n          if (orGate.op === 'OR' &&\n              ((orGate.in1 === gen && orGate.in2 === prop) || (orGate.in1 === prop && orGate.in2 === gen))) {\n            carries[n] = orGate.out;\n            if (orGate.out.startsWith('z') && orGate.out !== `z${(maxBit + 1).toString().padStart(2, '0')}`) {\n              swaps.add(orGate.out);\n            }\n            break;\n          }\n        }\n        break;\n      }\n    }\n  }\n  \n  // Final carry should be z{maxBit+1}\n  const finalCarry = carries[maxBit];\n  const zFinal = `z${(maxBit + 1).toString().padStart(2, '0')}`;\n  if (finalCarry && finalCarry !== zFinal) {\n    const fGate = wireOutputOf.get(zFinal);\n    if (fGate && fGate.out === zFinal) {\n      // zFinal is produced by some gate, should be the carry\n      swaps.add(finalCarry);\n      swaps.add(zFinal);\n    }\n  }\n  \n  return [...swaps].sort();\n}\n```\n\nHmm, this approach has some issues. Let me think more carefully.\n\nThe main issue is that I'm being too rigid in my tracing. When a swap happens, the expected pattern breaks and my trace might fail to find the OR gate (because it's looking for it with specific inputs that may be swapped). Let me think about a better approach.\n\nActually, I think the most reliable approach is a combination of:\n1. Structural validation to find suspicious wires\n2. Actually testing with inputs to verify\n\nLet me take a step back and think about what approach would be most correct.\n\n**Approach: Simulation-based validation of z-bits**\n\n1. Generate test inputs for the circuit:\n   - Test each x-bit in isolation: for each N, set xNN=1, everything else 0, y=0\n   - Test each y-bit in isolation: for each N, set yNN=1, everything else 0, x=0\n   - Some combined tests\n\n2. For each test case, simulate and check which z-bits don't match X+Y.\n\n3. The z-bits that fail give us information about which bit positions have swaps.\n\n4. For each failed bit position N, examine the gates associated with bit N:\n   - XOR(xNN, yNN) -> psum\n   - AND(xNN, yNN) -> gen\n   - The XOR/AND/OR gates that connect psum and carry\n   - The zNN output\n\n5. Cross-reference failed bits to find the swapped wires.\n\nActually, I just realized there's a much simpler approach that works well:\n\n**Approach: Rule-based validation of all gates**\n\nFor each gate, check if it follows the rules of a correct adder. Any wire used in violation of the rules is swapped.\n\nRules:\n1. An XOR gate must either:\n   a. Have inputs xNN and yNN (same bit) AND output to an internal wire (or z00 for N=0), OR\n   b. Have non-x/y inputs AND output to a zNN wire\n\n2. An AND gate must NOT output to a zNN wire.\n\n3. An OR gate must NOT output to a zNN wire (except possibly the final carry).\n\nWait, but this only catches some swaps. Let me think again...\n\nActually, I think the cleanest approach is:\n\n**Approach: build expected structure bottom-up, collect inconsistencies**\n\nStep 1: Identify all \"half-adder\" gates (xNN, yNN inputs):\n- XOR(xNN, yNN) -> psum[N]\n- AND(xNN, yNN) -> gen[N]\n\nThese are always present for all N (they're not swapped since their inputs come from x/y).\n\nStep 2: The psum wires and gen wires for each bit are known.\n\nStep 3: Now, for the structure connecting these, look at how the carry chain works:\n\nI know:\n- `carryIn[N]` should come from the OR of (gen[N-1], prop[N-1]) where prop[N-1] comes from AND(psum[N-1], carryIn[N-1])\n- `z[N]` should come from XOR(psum[N], carryIn[N])\n\nFor N=0:\n- z00 = psum[0]\n- carry[0] = gen[0]\n\nFor N>0:\n- I need to find XOR(psum[N], carry[N-1]) -> zNN\n- If I find this XOR and its output is NOT zNN, then its output is swapped with zNN\n- If I DON'T find this XOR, then zNN must come from some other gate, meaning something is swapped\n\nLet me trace through more carefully:\n\nFor N=1:\n- psum[1] = output of XOR(x01, y01)\n- gen[1] = output of AND(x01, y01)\n- carry[0] = gen[0] (or gen[0] if bit 0 works correctly)\n\nNow, what if z01 is swapped with gen[1] (a01 in the example)?\n\nExpected:\n- XOR(x01, y01) -> psum = t01\n- XOR(t01, carry0) -> z01\n- AND(x01, y01) -> gen = a01\n- AND(t01, carry0) -> b01 (prop)\n- OR(a01, b01) -> c01 (carry)\n\nAfter swap:\n- XOR(x01, y01) -> t01 (same)\n- XOR(t01, carry0) -> a01 (WRONG: should output to z01)\n- AND(x01, y01) -> z01 (WRONG: should output to a01)\n- AND(t01, carry0) -> b01 (same)\n- OR(a01, b01) -> c01 (same? Wait, a01 is now the XOR output, not the AND output)\n\nHmm, actually a01 is now both:\n1. The output of AND(x01, y01) is no longer a01, it's z01\n2. The output of XOR(t01, carry0) is a01\n\nAnd z01 is the output of AND(x01, y01). So z01 gets the wrong value.\n\nThe OR gate: OR(a01, b01) -> c01\n- a01 is now the XOR output, not the AND output\n- b01 is still AND(t01, carry0)\n- So OR gets: (t01 XOR carry0) AND (t01 AND carry0)\n- This should give carry anyway (since for full adder, sum XOR carry = partial XOR carry, and generate OR propagate = carry)\n\nWait no, OR((t01 XOR carry0), (t01 AND carry0)) = t01 OR carry0 (because t01 XOR carry0 = t01'*carry0 + t01*carry0', and t01 AND carry0 = t01*carry0, so OR gives t01*carry0' + t01*carry0 + t01'*carry0 = t01 + carry0). But a01 in the original circuit was AND(x01, y01), not XOR output. So the OR gate is now getting (XOR of psum and carry) OR (AND of psum and carry) instead of (AND of x01,y01) OR (AND of psum and carry).\n\nThis means the carry chain is now incorrect.\n\nOK, so in this scenario:\n- The XOR(t01, carry0) -> a01 doesn't output to z01. So a01 is \"wrong\" (it should be z01).\n- The AND(x01, y01) -> z01 outputs to z01 but should be a01. So z01 is \"wrong\" (it's coming from AND instead of XOR), and a01 is also \"wrong\" (it's from XOR instead of AND, and it goes to the wrong place).\n\nSo the swapped wires are: a01 (is used in OR but should be z01) and z01 (is from AND but should be from XOR).\n\nIn the swap set: {a01, z01}