Let me fix this. The fix is to calculate initial `needs` correctly:\n\n```typescript\nfor (const g of gates) {\n    let n = 0;\n    if (!(g.in1 in values)) n++;\n    if (!(g.in2 in values)) n++;\n    needs.set(g, n);\n    if (n === 0) queue.push(g);\n}\n```\n\nThis way, if both inputs are already available, needs = 0 and the gate is queued. If one input is available, needs = 1 (need to wait for the other). If neither is available, needs = 2.\n\nWhen a gate output becomes available, its consumers need - 1. When needs reaches 0, the consumer is queued.\n\nThis is correct! The issue was that I was setting needs = 2 for all gates regardless of whether their inputs were already available.\n\nLet me fix this in the simulate function.","signature":"531f6e04-eb7e-4422-b05d-15daf4394d84"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":714,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0,"cache_read_input_tokens":58752,"output_tokens":0,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b1556605-6ab7-400a-abeb-539ac462fe2c","uuid":"b0b2539e-d55d-4a8b-b0fa-06615d01d8ee"}
